<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Monroe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Monroe was the fifth President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1817 to 1825, after earlier posts as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and secretary of state. His presidency saw the acquisition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and the Monroe Doctrine of 1823, which warned European powers against new colonisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, the capital of Liberia, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-monroe.jpg" alt="James Monroe" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Finley Breese Morse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Monroe_White_House_portrait_1819_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a failed first attempt on Sherbro Island in 1820, the society’s agents acquired land at Cape Mesurado in December 1821, where from 1822 the settlement later named Monrovia, after President <b>James Monroe</b>, was established.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlement was named Monrovia after President <b>James Monroe</b>, and the colony was named Liberia in 1824.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-liberia">Colony of Liberia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monroe Hall, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-76.99&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Monroe Hall on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2634 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Monroe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3084 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3084 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-76.99&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the American continents, by the free and independent condition which they have assumed and maintain, are henceforth not to be considered as subjects for future colonization by any European powers.&#8221;</p><footer>Annual message to Congress (the Monroe Doctrine), 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/monroe.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we should consider any attempt on their part to extend their system to any portion of this hemisphere as dangerous to our peace and safety.&#8221;</p><footer>Annual message to Congress (the Monroe Doctrine), 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/monroe.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;National honor is national property of the highest value. The sentiment in the mind of every citizen is national strength. It ought therefore to be cherished.&#8221;</p><footer>First Inaugural Address, 1817 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/925/pg925-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
